course: dictionary
lesson: 3971
title: Fuzzy words and fuzzy meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Fuzzy has more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one. «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear.» Here fuzzy describes how the mould looks: covered with tiny hairs, not smooth.
explain: Fuzzy can also mean not clear. «Real borders in 1337 were often fuzzy zones» means the lines were not sharp or exact, not that the land was covered in hair. Ask yourself: does this meaning fit the sentence? If not, try another one.
explain: Fuzzy is also used for thinking. «My memory is fuzzy» means you cannot remember clearly. «Warm and fuzzy» is a pleasant, cosy feeling. The words around fuzzy are always the clues.
fact: Fuzzy means covered with soft, short hair or fibres, like a peach or a teddy bear.
fact: Fuzzy also means not clear or sharp, like a fuzzy picture or fuzzy sound.
fact: Fuzzy can describe thinking too: «my memory is fuzzy» means you cannot remember clearly.
fact: «Warm and fuzzy» means a pleasant, cosy feeling.
fact: «Watch for invented prices and fuzzy words.» Fuzzy words in a deal are unclear ones that can hide the real meaning.
exercise: «Leave a slice of bread in a warm, damp place and in a few days fuzzy spots of mould appear.» What does fuzzy describe here? | A feeling of comfort | A bright, clear picture | Sounds that are hard to hear | *Soft, short hairs covering the mould
exercise: A girl in Florida sees a fuzzy green spot on her bread and throws the whole loaf away. Why? | The bread is too soft to eat | She wants a new loaf for a picnic | Green spots are a kind of fruit | *Mould spreads threads you cannot see
exercise: «Real borders in 1337 were often fuzzy zones.» What does fuzzy mean here? | *Not clear or exact | Warm and cosy | Covered with soft hair | Very happy
test: «My memory of the trip is fuzzy.» What does fuzzy mean? | *Confused, not clear in your thinking | Cold and wet | Soft and furry | Bright and sharp
test: Mia says «warm and fuzzy» after the hug. What does it mean? | A blurry picture on the TV | A hot drink on a cold day | *A pleasant, cosy feeling | A soft towel from the washing line
exam: Dad says, «Watch for invented prices and fuzzy words» before buying a phone. What should you do? | Sign at once without reading the deal | Trust any price the seller says | Ask the seller to make the words fuzzier | *Say the deal back out loud, exactly, to check it
